Fix get_comoving_a() aborting on a time that is exactly old_a_time,
in a single-precision (AMReX_PRECISION=SINGLE) build.

Nyx aborts with

    amrex::Error::0::get_comoving_a: invalid time

partway through a Sedov run, AFTER every plotfile/dump for that step has
already been written, so the data is intact and only the exit status is lost.
Reproduced on AMReX 26.08 / Nyx 4ecfea2a85b9 at 64^3 and 128^3.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY WRONG

get_comoving_a() decides "is `time` at one end of the current a-interval" with

    const Real eps = 0.0001 * (new_a_time - old_a_time);
    ... else if (time > old_a_time - eps && time < old_a_time + eps)

eps is 1e-4 of the interval WIDTH, but it is then added to the interval's
ABSOLUTE POSITION. Those have wildly different magnitudes late in a run:
instrumented at the abort, with Real = float,

    time       = 0.13994413614273071
    old_a_time = 0.13994413614273071      <- time - old_a_time is EXACTLY 0
    new_a_time = 0.14000000059604645
    eps        = 5.5864455e-09

One float ULP at 0.14 is 2^-26 ~ 1.49e-8, so eps is below HALF an ulp.
`old_a_time - eps` and `old_a_time + eps` both round straight back to
old_a_time, and the test degenerates to

    time > old_a_time && time < old_a_time

which is empty. A `time` exactly equal to old_a_time -- the commonest query
there is, since the source terms ask for a at the start of every step -- then
misses that branch, misses the new_a_time branch (it is a full dt away), and
misses `time > old_a_time && time < new_a_time` on the same strict `>`.
Every branch falls through to amrex::Error().

This is why the abort looks nondeterministic: it fires only when a step
sequence happens to land a query exactly on old_a_time, so it depends on
resolution and on the problem parameters. Measured before this patch, all with
stop_time=0.13: 64^3/exp_energy=1 exits 0, but 64^3/exp_energy=3, 128^3/
exp_energy=1 and 128^3/exp_energy=3 all abort -- and the last of those aborted
at t=0.125, BELOW the stop_time it was given.

Double-precision builds are unaffected: a double ULP at 0.14 is ~2.8e-17, far
under eps, so the window is always representable there. That is why this has
survived -- SINGLE is the unusual configuration.

THE FIX

  * Floor eps at a few ulp of the interval's own magnitude, so the tolerance
    window is always at least representable in whatever precision Real is. In
    double precision the original 1e-4*dt term still dominates and nothing
    changes.
  * Make the endpoint comparisons inclusive. time == old_a_time is a legitimate
    query with an obvious answer, and strict inequalities are exactly what
    excluded it once eps underflowed.

VERIFIED

  * Seven configurations that aborted before (64^3 and 128^3, exp_energy 1 and
    3, stop_time 0.10/0.13/0.14/0.16/0.2) all exit 0 and run to their full
    stop_time.
  * The one configuration that already passed (64^3, exp_energy=1,
    stop_time=0.13) still passes at the identical step count and final time.
  * No physics change: 126/126 raw field dumps from the default benchmark run
    (128^3, 200 steps) are BIT-IDENTICAL to the same run on the unpatched
    binary.

diff --git a/Source/Driver/comoving.cpp b/Source/Driver/comoving.cpp
index 1433967..eccb909 100644
--- a/Source/Driver/comoving.cpp
+++ b/Source/Driver/comoving.cpp
@@ -1,3 +1,5 @@
+#include <algorithm>
+#include <limits>
 #include <iomanip>
 #include <Nyx.H>
 #include <constants_cosmo.H>
@@ -485,7 +487,25 @@ Nyx::comoving_est_time_step (Real& cur_time, Real& estdt)
 Real
 Nyx::get_comoving_a (Real time)
 {
-    const Real eps         = 0.0001 * (new_a_time - old_a_time);
+    // The tolerance for "time is at one end of the current a-interval".
+    //
+    // 1.e-4 * dt alone is not usable in a SINGLE-PRECISION build. dt here is
+    // ~5.6e-5 against times of ~0.14, so eps comes out ~5.6e-9 while one float
+    // ULP at 0.14 is ~1.5e-8 -- eps is below HALF an ulp, so both
+    // `old_a_time - eps` and `old_a_time + eps` round straight back to
+    // old_a_time and the window below collapses to the empty interval
+    // (old_a_time, old_a_time). A `time` that is EXACTLY old_a_time then fails
+    // that test, fails the new_a_time test, fails `time > old_a_time` in the
+    // interpolation branch, and aborts with "invalid time" -- for a time that
+    // is precisely the left endpoint of the interval it was asked about.
+    //
+    // Floor it at a few ulp of the interval's own magnitude so the window is
+    // always at least representable. In double precision the first term keeps
+    // dominating and behaviour is unchanged.
+    const Real eps         = std::max(Real(0.0001) * (new_a_time - old_a_time),
+                                      Real(4.0) * std::numeric_limits<Real>::epsilon()
+                                          * std::max(std::abs(old_a_time),
+                                                     std::abs(new_a_time)));
 
     // Test on whether time == old_a_time == new_a_time -- for example after restart
     //   before a has been integrated for the new time step.
@@ -495,15 +515,15 @@ Nyx::get_comoving_a (Real time)
     {
         return old_a;
     }
-    else if (time > old_a_time - eps && time < old_a_time + eps)
+    else if (time >= old_a_time - eps && time <= old_a_time + eps)
     {
         return old_a;
     }
-    else if (time > new_a_time - eps && time < new_a_time + eps)
+    else if (time >= new_a_time - eps && time <= new_a_time + eps)
     {
         return new_a;
     }
-    else if (time > old_a_time && time < new_a_time)
+    else if (time >= old_a_time && time <= new_a_time)
     {
         Real frac = (time - old_a_time) / (new_a_time - old_a_time);
         Real    a = frac*new_a + (1.0-frac)*old_a;
